import java.util.*;
import java.lang.*;

public class Solution {

    /**
     * Performs modular exponentiation to compute (g^n) % p.
     * This is the core operation for the Diffie-Hellman key exchange.
     * Using the binary exponentiation algorithm (exponentiation by squaring)
     * ensures the computation remains efficient even for large values of n.
     */
    public int modp(int g, int n, int p) {
        long result = 1;
        long base = g % p;
        long exponent = n;

        while (exponent > 0) {
            if ((exponent % 2) == 1) {
                result = (result * base) % p;
            }
            base = (base * base) % p;
            exponent = exponent / 2;
        }
        return (int) result;
    }
}

The implementation provided above utilizes the efficient binary exponentiation method to calculate the result of the expression (g raised to the power of n) modulo p. In the context of cryptography and the Diffie-Hellman key exchange, this specific function is vital because it allows systems to generate shared secret keys over insecure communication channels without ever transmitting the secret exponents themselves.

When we consider the mathematical operation (g^n) % p, the primary challenge is that calculating the full value of g^n would result in an integer that rapidly exceeds the storage capacity of standard 32-bit or even 64-bit primitive types. For instance, if g is 10 and n is 100, the resulting number is massive. By applying the modulo operator at each step of the multiplication process, we ensure that our intermediate values never exceed the capacity of a long integer, thus preventing overflow while maintaining numerical correctness.

The exponentiation by squaring algorithm reduces the time complexity of this operation from O(n) to O(log n). This logarithmic efficiency is the bedrock of modern asymmetric encryption. Without this capability, the computational cost of performing cryptographic handshakes would be prohibitive for modern hardware. The logic works by decomposing the exponent n into its binary representation. For every bit set in the binary form of the exponent, we perform a multiplication, and at every iteration, we square the base. 

In Java, we must be careful with data types. Although the method signature requests integers, using long for the base and result calculations is necessary to prevent overflow during the multiplication (base * base) % p before the modulo is applied. If we were to use standard integers for these intermediate steps, a result might be mathematically incorrect due to wrapping around the maximum integer limit. 

The security of the Diffie-Hellman protocol relies on the difficulty of the discrete logarithm problem. While calculating (g^n) % p is easy for a computer, determining n given the result and the base is computationally infeasible for sufficiently large primes. This makes it an ideal mechanism for establishing secure connections in protocols like TLS. By adhering to the constraints of the prime field p, we keep the arithmetic bounded and secure. The END.

